<v Police Officer 9>我希望能相信你，但在 22 年后，你会震惊我找到了什么以及我在哪里找到它。

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:53.010
<v Brian Reed>As for the cops, they're having trouble figuring out the truth. I heard the supervising officer on the scene, a sergeant, whisper to officer Gordy that he thought they should arrest both Charday and Thomas for fighting. Almost an hour passed. Inside, the officers continued to look through Charday's things, and then-- I'm not even sure if any of the cops noticed this-- a few tears began to run down Charday's face. Not long after, in a hamper full of dirty clothes in the closet, they found a gun, a long black revolver, exactly what both Thomas and Trina had independently described. It had one spent bullet.
<v Brian Reed>至于警察，他们在弄清楚真相方面遇到了麻烦。我听到现场的督导警官，一名中士，低声对戈迪警官说，他认为他们应该因为打架逮捕 Charday 和 Thomas。差不多过了一个小时。在里面，警察继续查看 Charday 的东西，然后——我甚至不确定是否有警察注意到这一点——几滴泪水开始顺着 Charday 的脸流下。没过多久，在壁橱里的一筐脏衣服里，他们发现了一把枪，一把长黑左轮手枪，正是 Thomas 和 Trina 独立描述的东西。它有一颗耗尽的子弹。

00:47:53.010 --> 00:48:05.770
<v Brian Reed>The cops arrested Charday for recklessly endangering safety by use of a firearm. Officer Gordy let her change out of her pajamas into warmer clothes, made sure her grandma was able to watch her kids, and then walked her out of the building in handcuffs.
<v Brian Reed>警察因使用枪支鲁莽危及安全逮捕了 Charday。戈迪警官让她从睡衣换成更暖和的衣服，确保她奶奶能看住她的孩子，然后把她戴着手铐走出大楼。

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:09.700
<v Richard Gordy>The kids are all taken care of and everything? It's OK that they're going to be with Grandma?
<v Richard Gordy>孩子们都照顾好了，一切都好了吗？他们和奶奶在一起没问题吧？

00:48:09.700 --> 00:48:10.090
<v Charday>Yeah.
<v Charday>是的。

00:48:10.090 --> 00:48:21.200
<v Richard Gordy>Are you crying?
<v Richard Gordy>你在哭吗？

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:26.740
<v Brian Reed>As all this was wrapping up, Trina called me over. She said she had something to say about the police.
<v Brian Reed>当所有这一切都结束时，Trina 叫我过去。她说她有关于警察的话要说。

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:46.010
<v Trina>You see how they do us? They don't believe us. Somebody could have been dead. You feel me? They was trying to say we were a liar, I mean, not [INAUDIBLE] was a liar, but they were trying to make it seem like it wasn't nothing when it was something. She shot at me and my boyfriend and five kids in the car.
<v Trina>你看到他们如何对待我们了吗？他们不相信我们。有人本可能死掉。你感觉到吗？他们试图说我们是骗子，我的意思是，不（听不清）是骗子，但他们试图让它看起来好像没什么事，当它有事时。她向我和我男朋友以及车里的五个孩子开枪。

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:56.620
<v Brian Reed>Trina pointed to a group of guys who had been hanging around the block while the investigation was going on. She told me they were Charday's people, cousins, and that Charday had threatened that they would go after Trina and her boyfriend.
<v Brian Reed>Trina 指着一群在调查进行时在街区徘徊的家伙。她告诉我他们是 Charday 的人，表兄弟，而且 Charday 威胁说他们会去找 Trina 和她男朋友。

00:48:56.620 --> 00:49:09.210
<v Trina>That's how people end up dead. When you call the police, you're being a snitch for real. Police got to take cautions right then and there. If they don't, when they leave, people get killed like that. You know what I'm saying? That's what I'm telling the police.
<v Trina>人们就是这样死掉的。当你打电话给警察时，你真的是在当告密者。警察必须当时就采取预防措施。如果他们不这样做，当他们离开时，人们就像那样被杀掉。你知道我正在说什么吗？这就是我正在告诉警察的。

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:13.470
<v Brian Reed>So you were worried that the police weren't going to believe you, they weren't going to find a gun.
<v Brian Reed>所以你担心警察不会相信你，他们不会找到枪。

00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:17.820
<v Trina>And they were going to leave and I was going to be here by myself and they were going to start some other stuff up.
<v Trina>而且他们会离开，我将独自留在这里，他们会开始其他事情。

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:19.970
<v Brian Reed>I mean, it seemed like it worked out, though.
<v Brian Reed>我的意思是，它看起来确实成功了，不过。

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:34.390
<v Trina>Yeah, it did, but they just really weren't believing us today, [INAUDIBLE] the gun, but that's just history, anyway. These white thugs, they judge you on how you look.
<v Trina>是的，它确实，但他们今天真的不相信我们，（听不清）枪，但这只是历史，无论如何。这些白人暴徒，他们根据你的外表来评判你。

00:49:34.390 --> 00:50:19.550
<v Brian Reed>Cops need to be skeptical during investigations. It's built into their jobs. They aren't supposed to fully trust the person they're talking to. But of course, the person they're talking to can feel that. Trina and Thomas did. And it's upsetting if you've just been shot at to have the police come and interrogate you like you're the criminal. And on top of that, there's the history, all the times the cops haven't treated people fairly or worse. That's always there, casting a shadow over every interaction. The cops feel like they're just doing their job the way they always do, but for Trina, it's hard not to wonder if the cops would be treating her this way if she was white.
<v Brian Reed>警察需要在调查期间持怀疑态度。这是他们工作的一部分。他们不应该完全相信他们正在交谈的人。但当然，他们正在交谈的人可以感觉到。Trina 和 Thomas 感到了。如果你刚刚被射击而有警察来并且像你是罪犯一样审问你，那是令人沮丧的。而且最重要的是，有历史，所有警察没有公平对待人们或者更糟的时候。那总是存在的，在每一次互动上投下阴影。警察觉得他们只是在做他们一直做的工作，但对于 Trina 来说，如果她是白人，很难不怀疑警察是否会这样对待她。

00:50:19.550 --> 00:51:05.880
<v Brian Reed>One last story. On April 30 last year, a white Milwaukee police officer shot and killed a black man named Dontre Hamilton. He was sleeping in a plaza across from City Hall in the afternoon, and an employee from a nearby Starbucks called the police. Two officers checked on Hamilton and determined he wasn't doing anything wrong. Then a different cop, the beat cop for the area, Officer Christopher Manny, came and roused him and tried to pat him down. This led to some kind of altercation. According to officer Manny and some witnesses, Hamilton grabbed his baton and hit him in the neck with it. Manny says he feared for his life. He shot Hamilton 14 times. Hamilton was 31 years old and had suffered from schizophrenia.
<v Brian Reed>最后一个故事。去年 4 月 30 日，一名白人密尔沃基警官枪杀了一名黑人男子，名叫 Dontre Hamilton。他下午睡在市政厅对面的广场上，附近一家星巴克的员工报了警。两名警官检查了 Hamilton 并确定他没做错任何事。然后另一个警察，该地区的巡警，Christopher Manny 警官，来叫醒他并试图搜身。这导致了某种争执。根据 Manny 警官和一些证人，Hamilton 抓住了他的警棍并用它击中了他的脖子。Manny 说他担心自己的生命。他射击 Hamilton 14 次。Hamilton 31 岁，患有精神分裂症。

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:40.320
<v Brian Reed>And unlike after the death of Derek Williams, the man in the back of a squad car who couldn't breathe, Chief Flynn fired officer Manny, though he didn't fire him for killing Hamilton. He fired him for doing a pat down that was against department policy and not in line with his training. Flynn says Officer Manny could tell when he approached that Hamilton was in the midst of a mental health issue, which means he should have never tried to touch him. That's rule number one when dealing with someone in a mental health crisis. And by trying to pat him down, Flynn says, Manny created the situation in which he feared for his life and ended up killing Dontre Hamilton.
<v Brian Reed>而且与在警车后座喘不过气的德里克·威廉姆斯死后不同，弗林局长解雇了 Manny 警官，尽管他并不是因为杀死了 Hamilton 而解雇他。他解雇他是因为他进行了违背部门政策且不符合他训练的搜身。弗林说 Manny 警官在他靠近时可以看出 Hamilton 正处于心理健康问题中，这意味着他根本不应该试图触摸他。在处理处于心理健康危机的人时，那是第一条规则。通过试图搜身，弗林说，Manny 创造了他担心自己生命并最终杀死了 Dontre Hamilton 的局面。

00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:41.810
<v Man 1>It was [INAUDIBLE].
<v Man 1>那是（听不清）。

00:51:41.810 --> 00:51:47.280
<v Man 2>It was murder. It was murder!
<v Man 2>那是谋杀。那是谋杀！

00:51:47.280 --> 00:52:10.180
<v Brian Reed>In November, after Officer Manny appealed Chief Flynn's decision to fire him, the public was given a chance to comment before Milwaukee's fire and police commission. I was there. People packed into a senior center gymnasium. On the right, members of the police union, almost entirely white. On the left, Dontre Hamilton's family and supporters, largely black and brown.
<v Brian Reed>11 月，在 Manny 警官上诉弗林局长解雇他的决定后，公众有机会在密尔沃基消防和警察委员会之前发表评论。我在那儿。人们挤进了一个老年中心体育馆。右边，工会成员，几乎全是白人。左边，Dontre Hamilton 的家人和支持者，主要是黑人和棕色人。

00:52:10.180 --> 00:52:49.890
<v Brian Reed>For an hour, Chief Flynn and the commission sat silently on stage as supporters of Dontre Hamilton berated them. They were angry that Flynn had taken six months to make a decision about Manny's firing, that he'd announced it two days after Manny had filed for disability, which meant he could still get paid, and that under a new state law, what was supposed to be an independent investigation into Dontre Hamilton's death had been done by former Milwaukee police officers. On the other side, the police union members were furious that Flynn would even consider suspending Officer Manny, never mind firing him, for doing what union president Mike Crivello said any reasonable officer would do in that situation.
<v Brian Reed>有一个小时，弗林局长和委员会默默地坐在台上，当 Dontre Hamilton 的支持者责骂他们时。他们很愤怒，因为弗林花了六个月时间才做出关于 Manny 解雇的决定，他在 Manny 申请残疾两天后宣布了它，这意味着他仍然可以拿到报酬，并且根据一项新的州法律，本来应该是对 Dontre Hamilton 死亡的独立调查是由前密尔沃基警察做的。另一方面，工会成员很愤怒，弗林竟然考虑停职 Manny 警官，更不用说解雇他了，因为做工会主席 Mike Crivello 所说的任何理性的警察在那种情况下都会做的事。

00:52:49.890 --> 00:53:23.620
<v Mike Crivello>When the situation created the necessity for the use of a deadly force, PO Manny did what he had to. Certainly would have rather not have had to. Certainly would have rather not have had to. We are grateful he survived. I am 100% confident that a [INAUDIBLE] tragedy. We are saddened by the loss of Mr. Hamilton. We have compassion for the family. I am praying for peace for the Hamilton family.
<v Mike Crivello>当情况创造了使用致命武力的必要性时，PO Manny 做了他必须做的事。当然本来更愿意没必须那样做。当然本来更愿意没必须那样做。我们很感激他幸存下来。我 100% 确信那是（听不清）悲剧。我们对 Hamilton 先生的损失感到悲伤。我们对家人有同情心。我正在为 Hamilton 家人的和平祈祷。

00:53:23.620 --> 00:53:28.120
<v Mike Crivello>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Mike Crivello>（交叉发言）

00:53:28.120 --> 00:54:03.590
<v Brian Reed>There was one thing both sides could agree on. They both wanted Chief Flynn gone. Dontre Hamilton's supporters had issued a demand for his resignation, and the union had recently held a no confidence vote, where 99% of the cops who showed up had voted against the chief. At one point during the public comments, Chief Flynn began to look down at his lap. He was checking this phone. People shouted at him, saying how dare he, saying he was being insensitive. After the meeting, in the lobby of the senior center as Flynn was on his way out, he addressed it with a scrum of reporters.
<v Brian Reed>有一件事双方可以达成一致。他们都想要弗林局长离开。Dontre Hamilton 的支持者发出了辞职要求，工会最近举行了不信任投票，99% 到场的警察投票反对局长。在公众评论期间，弗林局长开始看着他的膝盖。他正在查看这个电话。人们对他大喊大叫，说他怎么敢，说他不够敏感。会议结束后，在大厅里，当弗林离开时，他用一群记者解决了它。

00:54:03.590 --> 00:54:21.200
<v Ed Flynn>Well, I was on my phone, and yes, that's true. I was following developments with a five-year-old little girl sitting on her dad's lap who had just got shot in the head by a drive by shooting. And if some of the people here gave a good goddamn about the victimization of people in this community by crime, I'd take some of their invective more seriously.
<v Ed Flynn>嗯，我在用我的手机，是的，那是真的。我正在追踪一个坐在她爸爸膝盖上的五岁小女孩的进展，她刚被枪击中头部，是驾车枪击。如果这里有些人对这个社区的人们因犯罪而受害给予很好的上帝的诅咒，我会更认真地对待他们的一些辱骂。

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:50.610
<v Ed Flynn>The greatest racial disparity in the city of Milwaukee is getting shot and killed. Hello. 80% of my homicide victims every year are African American. 80% of our aggravated assault victims are African American. 80% of our shooting victims who survive their shooting are African American. Now, they know all about the last three people that have been killed by the Milwaukee police department over the course of the last several years. There's not one of them can name one of the last three homicide victims we've had in this city.
<v Ed Flynn>密尔沃基市最大的种族差异是被枪击并杀害。你好。每年 80% 的凶杀案受害者是非裔美国人。80% 的严重袭击受害者是非裔美国人。80% 幸存枪击的枪击受害者是非裔美国人。现在，他们知道过去几年密尔沃基警察部门杀害的最后三个人的一切。他们中没有一个人能说出我们在该市最近的三名凶杀案受害者中任何一个人的名字。

00:54:50.610 --> 00:55:13.950
<v Ed Flynn>Now, there's room for everybody to participate in fixing this police department, and I'm not pretending we're without sin, but this community is at risk, all right, and it's not because men and women in blue risk their lives protecting it. It's at risk because we've got large numbers of high capacity quality firearms in the hands of remorseless criminals who don't care who they shoot. Now, I'm leaving here to go to that scene.
<v Ed Flynn>现在，每个人都有参与修复这个警察部门的空间，我不是假装我们没有罪，但这个社区处于危险之中，好吧，它并不是因为蓝色的男人和女人冒着生命危险保护它。它处于危险之中，因为我们有大量高容量高质量的枪支在那些不关心他们射击谁的冷酷犯罪分子手中。现在，我要离开这里去那个现场。

00:55:13.950 --> 00:55:51.110
<v Ed Flynn>And I take it personally, OK? We're going up there and there's a bunch of cops processing a scene of a dead kid. They're the ones that are going to take the risks to their life to try to clean this thing up, all right? We are responsible for the things we get wrong and we take action. We've arrested cops, we've fired cops, and so on. But when you start getting yelled at for reading the updates of the kid that got shot, yeah, you take it personal, OK? Now no offense, but I'm going up there now.
<v Ed Flynn>而且我个人承担，好吗？我们正在去那儿，有一堆警察正在处理一个死小孩的现场。他们是那些将冒着生命危险试图清理这个东西的人，好吗？我们对我们做错的事情负责，并且我们采取行动。我们逮捕了警察，我们解雇了警察，等等。但当你开始因为阅读刚被枪击的孩子的更新而遭到责骂时，是的，你把它个人化，好吗？现在没冒犯，但我现在要去那儿了。

00:55:51.110 --> 00:56:13.840
<v Brian Reed>If you're going to repair the relationship between African Americans and the police, at least the way Chief Flynn is trying to, it's going to happen in the course of thousands of encounters between cops and black residents, thousands of encounters that need to go well, and even if they do, each one still only brings you one small step closer to building trust. It's a game of inches.
<v Brian Reed>如果你要修复非裔美国人与警察之间的关系，至少是弗林局长试图这样做的方式，它将在警察与黑人居民之间数千次接触的过程中发生，数千次需要进展顺利的接触，即使它们确实做到了，每一个仍然只带你向建立信任更近一小步。这是一场英寸的游戏。

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:52.510
<v Brian Reed>Right now, according to that University of Wisconsin survey, 63% of black Milwaukeeans say they're satisfied with police, versus 83% of white Milwaukeeans. Remember Trina, the woman whose neighbor shot at her and her boyfriend? On paper, her case looks like a success for the Milwaukee police. They arrested the shooter, confiscated the gun, and on top of that, they helped Trina out, offered to stand guard while Trina and her boyfriend moved out of the apartment building that day to make sure the neighbor's cousins didn't do anything to them. Trina thanked the officers before they left, and still, when we talked about if she trusted the Milwaukee PD, she said--
<v Brian Reed>现在，根据威斯康星大学的那项调查，63% 的黑人密尔沃基人说他们对警察满意，对比 83% 的白人密尔沃基人。还记得 Trina 吗，那个邻居向她和她男朋友开枪的女士？在纸面上，她的案例看起来是密尔沃基警察的成功。他们逮捕了枪手，没收了枪支，最重要的是，他们帮了 Trina，提议当 Trina 和她男朋友那天搬离公寓楼时站在岗哨，确保邻居的表兄弟不会对他们做什么。Trina 在他们离开前感谢了警官，仍然，当我们谈论她是否信任密尔沃基警察时，她说——

00:56:52.510 --> 00:56:58.370
<v Trina>I don't really. It's a 50/50 chance, you know what I'm saying? 50/50.
<v Trina>我真的不信任。这是 50/50 的机会，你知道我正在说什么吗？50/50。

00:56:58.370 --> 00:57:00.200
<v Brian Reed>50/50 chance of what?
<v Brian Reed>50/50 什么的机会？

00:57:00.200 --> 00:57:09.590
<v Trina>Like sometimes they help people, sometimes they wouldn't. You know what I'm saying? That was the grace of god that they found that gun.
<v Trina>就像有时他们帮助人们，有时他们不会。你知道我正在说什么吗？那是上帝的恩典，他们找到了那把枪。

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:11.360
<v Brian Reed>Sometimes they'll help you and sometimes they won't?
<v Brian Reed>有时他们会帮助你，有时他们不会？

00:57:11.360 --> 00:57:20.930
<v Trina>Yeah. They won't.
<v Trina>是的。他们不会。

00:57:20.930 --> 00:57:59.160
<v Brian Reed>Trina is not the only black Milwaukeean who said something like this to me. I asked one woman what she thought of the Milwaukee PD and she put it like this. You just don't know what to expect. That stayed with me. She wasn't saying all cops are racist or the whole force is corrupt. She was saying something much more measured, that the police are unpredictable, which means at least this woman felt that when you have an interaction with a cop, you can never be sure what you're going to get.
<v Brian Reed>Trina 不是唯一一个对我这样说的黑人密尔沃基人。我问一个女人她对密尔沃基警察有什么看法，她这样表达。你只是不知道会发生什么。那留在了我的脑子里。她并不是在说所有的警察都是种族主义者或者整个部队都是腐败的。她说了一些衡量得多的事情，警察是不可预测的，这意味着至少这个女人感到当你与警察进行互动时，你永远无法确定你会得到什么。

00:57:59.160 --> 00:58:30.720
<v Ira Glass>Brian Reed is one of the producers of our program. Ben Calhoun helped produce that story. Next week, some cities where policing is so different from Milwaukee in the second week of our series.
<v Ira Glass>布莱恩·里德是我们节目的制片人之一。本·卡尔霍恩帮忙制作了这个故事。下周，一些城市的警务与密尔沃基在我们的系列节目的第二周非常不同。

00:58:30.720 --> 00:58:56.720
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Brian Reed with Sean Cole, Stephanie [INAUDIBLE], Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Editing by Joel Lovell. Production help from Simon Adler. Seth Lind is our operations director. Emily Condon's our production manager. Elise Bergerson's our office manager. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website. Elna Baker scout stories for our show. Research help from Michelle Harris, Christopher Swetala, and Ben Anastas. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由布莱恩·里德与肖恩·科尔、斯蒂芬妮（听不清）、Chana Joffe-Walt、米奇·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克制作。我们的高级制片人是朱莉·斯奈德。由乔尔·洛弗尔编辑。西蒙·阿德勒的制作帮助。塞思·林德是我们的运营总监。艾米丽·康登是我们的制作经理。艾丽丝·伯格森是我们的办公室经理。阿德里安·马蒂奥维茨经营我们的网站。埃尔娜·贝克为我们的节目探查故事。米歇尔·哈里斯、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉和本·阿纳斯塔斯的调查帮助。来自达米安·格拉夫和罗布·格迪斯的音乐帮助。

00:58:56.720 --> 00:59:12.840
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>（致谢）

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:30.840
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX. the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks as always to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, he finally met a few of our listeners at a WBEZ event, and I don't know what he was thinking. They were very different from what he expected.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由 PRX——公共广播交流平台交付给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人，托雷·马拉蒂亚先生。你知道，他终于在一次 WBEZ 活动中见到我们的一些听众，我不知道他在想什么。他们和他预期的非常不同。

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:36.160
<v James Harpole>I see families, people pushing their babies. It shocked me. I was in shock.
<v James Harpole>我看到家庭，人们推着他们的婴儿。它震惊了我。我很震惊。

00:59:36.160 --> 00:59:41.160
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with part two of our series on policing and more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带着我们关于警务的系列节目的第二部分和更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
